---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CLOUD_HOST, SUPPORT_EMAIL, UPDATED } from "../config";
import { path } from "../i18n/ui";

const description =
  "Terms of use for Garage Opener: the app controls a physical door, the self-hosted bridge is yours to run, and the shared cloud-alerts service is best effort.";
---

<Base locale="en" route="terms" title="Terms" description={description} updated={UPDATED}>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Terms</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Short, because there isn't much to say. You bought an app that talks to hardware you own. Here is what
      that does and does not promise.
    </p>

    <h2>This app opens a physical door</h2>
    <p>
      That is worth saying plainly. A garage door is heavy machinery. Use the app only with an opener that has
      working safety features — a functioning photo-eye and auto-reverse — installed and maintained to its
      manufacturer's instructions and your local rules. Never operate the door when you cannot see that it is
      clear. Do not rely on the app, the sensor or the notifications as a safety device: they tell you what
      the sensor reported, which is not the same as a guarantee about what is under the door.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app is provided as is, without warranty of any kind. To the fullest extent the law allows, we are
      not liable for damage, injury or loss arising from the door operating, failing to operate, or reporting
      a state that turns out to be wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Your hardware and your network are yours</h2>
    <p>
      You are responsible for your UniFi Protect console, your relay wiring, your sensor, your network and the
      credentials you create. If you expose your bridge to the internet, how you do that is your decision and
      your responsibility — <a href={path("en", "selfHosting")}>our guidance is here</a>, and it is guidance,
      not a guarantee.
    </p>

    <h2>The self-hosted bridge</h2>
    <p>
      The bridge is software you run on your own machine. You choose the version, you keep it updated, you
      hold its tokens. It is offered on the same as-is basis as the app. Support for self-hosted setups is
      best effort — see <a href={path("en", "support")}>Support</a> for what that means in practice.
    </p>

    <h2>The cloud-alerts service</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{CLOUD_HOST}</code> is a small free service run on a hobby budget. It is <strong>best
      effort</strong>: no uptime commitment, no delivery guarantee, and it may be rate-limited, changed or
      discontinued. Notifications can be late or lost for reasons entirely outside it — your console not
      sending a webhook, Apple's push service, a phone with no signal.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not use it as the only thing standing between your garage and being left open. It is a helpful
      reminder, not a monitoring system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Abuse of the service — flooding it, trying to reach other people's installs, or using it for anything
      other than a garage door you own — will get your install blocked. <a href={path("en", "privacy")}>What
      it stores is here</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Not affiliated with Ubiquiti</h2>
    <p>
      “UniFi” and “UniFi Protect” are trademarks of Ubiquiti Inc. This app and this site are independent, and
      are not affiliated with, endorsed by or sponsored by Ubiquiti. Compatibility is stated as fact, not as a
      partnership. Ubiquiti may change Protect at any time in ways that break this app.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>These terms may change; the date below tells you when they last did.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>
